(2466402, 337360, 31794, '<td valign="top">[[trading post]]<br/> <br/>great picture, and it scored accordingly.<br/> <br/>when I first saw it, it reminded me of all the funny "warnings" you can find on American wrapings.. like on a bag of nuts "warning may contain nuts" and on a kitchen knife "warning knife may be sharp"<br/> <br/>to get a warning that the coffie you just bought might be hot is strange... you''d never buy it if it were cold ;)<br/> <br/>back to the picture... composition, lighting and subject are all very well thought of, and the picture is very good.<br/></td>', '[[trading post]] great picture, and it scored accordingly. when I first saw it, it reminded me of all the funny "warnings" you can find on American wrapings.. like on a bag of nuts "warning may contain nuts" and on a kitchen knife "warning knife may be sharp" to get a warning that the coffie you just bought might be hot is strange... you''d never buy it if it were cold ;) back to the picture... composition, lighting and subject are all very well thought of, and the picture is very good.', '2006-05-29 15:03:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(2466404, 337064, 57650, '<td valign="top">lovely sensational sexy image</td>', 'lovely sensational sexy image', '2006-05-29 15:04:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(2466408, 337152, 14414, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Melethia:</b></div><hr/><i> Whatever you did to remove background noise worked well - it''s very black and smooth.  Now, what is it?  :) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This is where I like advanced editing :) I selected the background loosely, basically selecting anything I wanted smooth while avoiding any detail, and leaving a slight gap between selection and the detailed areas. Then Filter &gt; Noise &gt; Median, at whatever radius works best.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-29 14:08:36.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by Melethia: Whatever you did to remove background noise worked well - it''s very black and smooth.  Now, what is it?  :)   This is where I like advanced editing :) I selected the background loosely, basically selecting anything I wanted smooth while avoiding any detail, and leaving a slight gap between selection and the detailed areas. Then Filter > Noise > Median, at whatever radius works best.', '2006-05-29 15:05:30.000000', '2006-05-29 15:08:36.000000', 0),
