(2473181, 336591, 40718, '<td valign="top">One thing I find is that you either want detail to your subject (the tower) or you want complete sillouette.  If you drag the photo into PS, you see the tone is about a 29 on a range of 0 to 255 (0=black, 255=white).  Use levels and increase the low number to about 30.  This will give the tower a true black (almost) and you can see it looks better.<br/> <br/>Next, for heat, the blue in the sky hurts.  It doesn''t mean it isn''t nice for an ordinary picture, but in the context of "heat" it cools things down.  Take your picture again in PS and adjust the color balance to +100/0/-100 (a bit extreme, but to prove a point).  This will turn all that nice blue to a orangy brown.  Now it looks like we need a glass of water.  Before I actually thought of a cold winter prairie sunset.</td>', 'One thing I find is that you either want detail to your subject (the tower) or you want complete sillouette.  If you drag the photo into PS, you see the tone is about a 29 on a range of 0 to 255 (0=black, 255=white).  Use levels and increase the low number to about 30.  This will give the tower a true black (almost) and you can see it looks better. Next, for heat, the blue in the sky hurts.  It doesn''t mean it isn''t nice for an ordinary picture, but in the context of "heat" it cools things down.  Take your picture again in PS and adjust the color balance to +100/0/-100 (a bit extreme, but to prove a point).  This will turn all that nice blue to a orangy brown.  Now it looks like we need a glass of water.  Before I actually thought of a cold winter prairie sunset.', '2006-05-31 18:39:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(2473294, 337429, 41337, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from CTP2</b> <br/> <br/>Ok, I must say this is pretty strange. The coat and hat certainly isn''t things you associate with heat and the reddish cheeks could also be something produced by the cold. However, even with the coat and hat I think this could have worked as a heat entry had the model looked really tired. For example, a full body shot with the hands on the knees and bending over which would have told me right there the person just completed a long hike or something and was exhausted. Or maybe not even a full body shot but just enough to show that type of pose and that way you''d keep the reddness in the cheeks prominent in the shot. So really the only issue for me here is she doesn''t look tired. Perhaps just spraying some water on her face would have been enough? All in all I like how you tried to do something different.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-05-31 18:17:32.</i></td>', 'Greetings from CTP2  Ok, I must say this is pretty strange. The coat and hat certainly isn''t things you associate with heat and the reddish cheeks could also be something produced by the cold. However, even with the coat and hat I think this could have worked as a heat entry had the model looked really tired. For example, a full body shot with the hands on the knees and bending over which would have told me right there the person just completed a long hike or something and was exhausted. Or maybe not even a full body shot but just enough to show that type of pose and that way you''d keep the reddness in the cheeks prominent in the shot. So really the only issue for me here is she doesn''t look tired. Perhaps just spraying some water on her face would have been enough? All in all I like how you tried to do something different.', '2006-05-31 19:16:01.000000', '2006-05-31 19:17:32.000000', 0),
